`$before` and `$after` add a prefix and suffix around each direct child template.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    list #= [$before = [:<li>], $after = [:</li>]:
        <ul>
            [$slot]
        </ul>
    ]

    [list:
        [: one]
        [: two]
    ]
]

Each direct child becomes one list item.

The composed order is prefix content, the direct child, then suffix content.
The child insertion point is implicit because only one is allowed.

### Values and named slots

Each directive is optional, and either may be used alone. Each may occur once
per head, so a repeated `$before` or `$after` is rejected. Both may appear in the
same head. The assignment marker is required, so `$before` or `$after` without
`=` is invalid.

A value is a compile-time String or a constant template wrapper. A plain String
is literal text. A compile-time String may also retain structural resource
pieces, which survive composition and resolve in the output context. A constant
template wrapper may contain named slots, which the enclosing application fills.
Its unresolved slot identity and structural resource pieces survive composition.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    row #= [$html:<tr style="[$slot row_style]">]
    rows #= [$before = row, $after = [$html:</tr>]:[$slot]]

    [rows:
        [$in row_style:background: red;]
        [: first]
        [: second]
    ]
]

The `$in row_style` contribution belongs to the enclosing `rows` application
and fills the wrapper's named slot. It is not routed to either child.

An occurrence can own a default in the wrapper value. The default applies only
when no contribution reaches that occurrence:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    rows #= [$before = [$html:<tr style="[$slot row_style = "background: white;"]">], $after = [$html:</tr>]:[$slot]]
]

Only named slots may appear in a `$before` or `$after` value. An explicit
default or positional slot anywhere in the value, including in a nested
template, is rejected at the offending slot. The child insertion point is implicit.

Runtime-dependent expressions and values that are neither compile-time Strings
nor constant template wrappers are rejected at their authored span. For a
template value, normal template body whitespace applies, including a space
immediately after its body colon.

### No output and loops

A false or absent condition and a zero-iteration loop emit no wrapper. A selected
empty String still counts as output and is wrapped. A direct child loop is
wrapped once around its aggregate output. Put a wrapper inside the loop body to
wrap each iteration.

### Direct-child scope

The wrapper applies only to direct children.

Literal body text is not individually wrapped.

A selected child becomes an opaque contribution to its parent.

The parent's wrapper does not descend into that child's nested templates.

A nested helper may declare its own `$before` and `$after` wrapper.

This keeps row, cell, list and layout helpers from leaking wrappers into deeper
content.

The same rules apply through const folding, runtime lowering, imports and
re-exports.

### `$fresh`

`$fresh` opts one child out of the immediate parent wrapper.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    [list:
        [: one]
        [$fresh: [: two]]
    ]
]

The first child receives the list-item wrapper.

The second child does not.

`$fresh`:

- affects one child
- affects only the immediate parent wrapper
- does not change sibling behaviour
- does not disable wrappers declared deeper inside the child
